# APZHUB Step 2 — Shell layout zones (locked)

This step locks the **physical structure** of APZHUB. The shell must be persistent and desktop-like so users do not feel they are bouncing between unrelated pages.

## 2.1 Global shell structure

The application layout must always be:

**Header**  
**Body area**

- Left primary rail  
- Optional secondary rail  
- Main canvas  
- Optional right utility panel  

**Footer**

Visually it behaves like a **desktop app frame**, not a website.

## 2.2 Header

The header is **always present**. High-value global controls only.

### Left

- APZHUB logo  
- Current workspace title or current context  
- Optional breadcrumb for deeper views  

### Center

- Global search bar  

Search should eventually cover: apps, users, tickets, projects, timesheets, documentation, mail metadata, calendar items.

### Right

- Quick actions menu  
- Notifications  
- App launcher  
- Linked account status  
- Profile/avatar  

Keep the header **clean**. Do not overload with stats, cards, or decorative text.

## 2.3 Footer

The footer is **persistent** and **subtle** — a system bar, not a marketing footer.

Include as appropriate:

- Environment label if needed  
- Connectivity/status indicator  
- Background sync/provisioning indicator  
- Current tenant or workspace context if relevant  
- Version/build info  
- Help/support entry  

## 2.4 Left primary rail

Main navigation anchor: **always present**, icon-led, labels visible when expanded.

**Top-level areas only** — never a dumping ground for every feature.

### End-user mode

- Home  
- My Work  
- Apps  
- Calendar  
- Mail  
- Search  
- Notifications  

### Admin mode

- Admin Home  
- Users  
- Access  
- Bundles  
- Services  
- Provisioning  
- Vault  
- Audit  
- Policies  

Support: collapse/expand, active state, pinning favorites later if needed.

## 2.5 Secondary rail

Appears **only when needed**. **Contextual**, never global.

Purpose: local navigation inside the selected area, filters, saved views, contextual actions.

Examples:

- **My Work:** Overview, Tickets, Projects, Timesheets  
- **Admin / Users:** All Users, Pending Users, Linked Accounts, Role Exceptions  
- **Services:** Internal, External, SSO Ready, Vault Managed, Provisioning Errors  

## 2.6 Main canvas

Core workspace. Must support:

- Single panel views  
- Two-column split views  
- Resizable panels  
- Detail drill-ins  
- Modals and drawers for secondary actions  
- Persistent state when moving around where practical  

Workbench feel: list + detail, summary + detail, matrix + inspector, dashboard summaries with launch panels. Most user time is spent here.

## 2.7 Right utility panel

**Optional** and **collapsible**. Purpose is **context**, not navigation. The screen must work without it.

**End users (examples):** today’s calendar, recent emails, reminders, activity context, item details.

**Admin (examples):** provisioning job status, audit details, connector health, selected user/service info, Vault access history.

## 2.8 End-user shell composition

Default:

- Header  
- Left rail  
- Main canvas  
- Right utility panel when useful  
- Footer  

Main canvas defaults: summary widgets (top or left), operational lists center, launch-focused actions; no heavy admin controls, no policy jargon, no clutter. Daily work and visibility.

## 2.9 Admin shell composition

Same structural shell; different emphasis.

Default:

- Header  
- Left rail  
- Secondary rail **often** active  
- Main canvas: tables, matrixes, provisioning queues  
- Right utility inspector panel  
- Footer  

Favor: dense but readable information, strong filters, list/detail, matrix layouts, operational status. Deliberate and controlled, not decorative.

## 2.10 Split-view rules

Allowed patterns (do not exceed **two main splits** at once — avoid noise):

- **Pattern A — List + detail:** left list, right detail  
- **Pattern B — Matrix + inspector:** main matrix, right inspector  
- **Pattern C — Summary + work area:** top summary, bottom live area  
- **Pattern D — Two operational panels:** resizable left and right panes  

## 2.11 Responsive behavior

Desktop-first; degrade cleanly on smaller screens:

- Left rail: icon-only or collapsible drawer  
- Right utility: slide-over  
- Footer: compact  
- Split views: stack  

Design priority: **desktop and laptop productivity**, not mobile-first.

## 2.12 Visual behavior rules

Premium shell:

- Header, footer, rails, panels feel like **one system**  
- Consistent spacing  
- Subtle borders  
- Clear panel hierarchy  
- Calm hover and active states (not flashy)  
- Minimal jumpy reflow between areas  

The frame must feel **dependable**.

## Locked Step 2 statement

**APZHUB uses a persistent desktop-style shell with a global header, system footer, left primary rail, optional secondary rail, central resizable work canvas, and optional right utility panel, with the same shell structure assembling into distinct end-user and admin experiences.**
